        "math_academy_id": "q-346820",
        "problem": "Express $\\frac{6{x}^{2}+x-3}{{x}^{2}(x-1)}$ as a sum of partial fractions.\n\n| $\\frac{6{x}^{2}+x-3}{{x}^{2}(x-1)}\\,=$ | {{field-1}} | $+$ | {{field-2}} | $+$ | {{field-3}} |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $x$ |  | ${x}^{2}$ |  | $x-1$ |",
        "worked_solution": "Our task is to find $A,$ $B,$ and $C$ such that\n\n$$\n\\frac{6{x}^{2}+x-3}{{x}^{2}(x-1)}=\\frac{A}{x}+\\frac{B}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{C}{x-1}.\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by ${x}^{2}(x-1)$ gives\n\n$$\n6{x}^{2}+x-3=Ax(x-1)+B(x-1)+C{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nLetting $x=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6(0)^{2}+(0)-3 & =A(0)(0-1)+B(0-1)+C(0)^{2} \\\\ -3 & =B(-1) \\\\ B & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x=1$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6(1)^{2}+(1)-3 & =A(1)(1-1)+B(1-1)+C(1)^{2} \\\\ 4 & =C(1)^{2} \\\\ 4 & =1C \\\\ C & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, letting $x=2$ and using our values for $B$ and $C$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6(2)^{2}+(2)-3 & =A(2)(2-1)+3(2-1)+4(2)^{2} \\\\ 23 & =A(2)(1)+3(1)+4(4) \\\\ 23 & =2A+3+16 \\\\ 23 & =2A+19 \\\\ A & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\frac{6{x}^{2}+x-3}{{x}^{2}(x-1)}=\\frac{2}{x}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{4}{x-1}.\n$$",
